// OS Notes - deep rewrite for all OS topics (detailed multi-paragraph notes)
module.exports = [
  { topic: 'OS Introduction', title: 'What is an Operating System?', content: 'An operating system is the layer between hardware and applications: it manages the CPU, memory, devices and files, and offers processes a clean, protected abstraction over raw hardware. Two broad jobs: resource manager (decides who gets CPU, RAM, disk, bandwidth, and arbitrates fairly and efficiently) and control program (supervises execution, prevents errors and improper use, provides system calls).\n\nKey services: process management (creation, scheduling, IPC), memory management (allocation, paging, virtual memory), file system management, I/O and device management via drivers, security and protection (user/kernel mode), and a user interface (shell or GUI). The kernel is the always-resident core; system calls are the gates into it.\n\nInterview framing: define the OS, then walk layer by layer - hardware -> kernel (scheduler, memory manager, VFS) -> system call interface -> libraries -> applications.', keyPoints: ['OS = resource manager + control program over hardware', 'Kernel runs in privileged (kernel) mode; user apps in user mode', 'System calls are the only legal entry into the kernel', 'Core duties: processes, memory, files, I/O, protection', 'Examples: Linux, Windows, macOS, Android'], commonMistakes: ['Calling the OS just software that runs apps - the management and protection roles are the essence', 'Mixing up kernel and shell - the shell is a user program on top of the OS'], interviewTips: ['Give the layered picture and name one service each layer provides', 'Be ready to explain kernel mode vs user mode with a system-call example'] },
  { topic: 'Types of OS', title: 'Types of Operating Systems', content: 'Operating systems are classified by how they handle users and jobs: batch (jobs queued without interaction, high throughput), multiprogramming (several jobs resident to keep the CPU busy during I/O), multitasking/time-sharing (rapid switching gives each user a slice, prioritising response time), multiprocessing (two or more CPUs share the workload), real-time (hard RT must meet deadlines - airbags, pacemakers; soft RT prefers to - video streaming), distributed (multiple networked machines act as one system) and embedded/mobile (resource-constrained, special purpose).\n\nThe classic contrast: multiprogramming maximises CPU utilisation by overlapping I/O and compute; time-sharing minimises response time by rotating the CPU quickly. Real-time adds determinism - the scheduler must guarantee deadlines, so algorithms are priority/preemption based with bounded jitter.\n\nGive one real example per type when answering: payroll batch, Linux time-sharing, VxWorks/QNX hard real-time, clusters for distributed.', keyPoints: ['Batch: no interaction, maximum throughput', 'Multiprogramming: many jobs resident, CPU stays busy', 'Time-sharing: quick rotation, interactive response time', 'Hard vs soft real-time: guaranteed vs best-effort deadlines', 'Distributed: many machines presented as one system'], commonMistakes: ['Saying multiprogramming and multitasking are identical - goals differ (CPU use vs response time)', 'Calling Windows a hard real-time OS - scheduling is not deadline-guaranteed'], interviewTips: ['Distinguish multiprogramming vs multitasking explicitly - the standard probe', 'Name a real example per type: batch payroll, Linux, VxWorks'] },
  { topic: 'Process', title: 'What is a Process?', content: 'A process is a program in execution: code plus the dynamic state it accumulates. Its address space has four regions: text (code), data (globals), heap (dynamic allocation, grows upward) and stack (frames, return addresses, grows downward). A program is passive - a file on disk; a process is active - the program plus program counter, registers, stack, open files and resources.\n\nCreation is hierarchical: fork() clones the calling process (copy-on-write makes it cheap), exec() replaces the image with a new program; the creator is a parent, the created a child. On termination a child reports a status to the parent via wait(); orphans get reparented to init, zombies wait to be reaped.\n\nThe OS tracks each process in a PCB and switches between them via context switches. Threads are lighter-weight execution units inside a process - the usual follow-up question.', keyPoints: ['Process = program in execution with text, data, heap, stack', 'fork() clones, exec() replaces, wait() reaps the child', 'Copy-on-write makes fork cheap until pages are written', 'Zombie: terminated child not yet waited on; orphan: parent died first', 'The kernel represents each process with a PCB'], commonMistakes: ['Saying a program and a process are the same - one program can spawn many processes', 'Forgetting that fork returns twice (0 in child, child PID in parent)'], interviewTips: ['Sketch the four address-space regions and say which way each grows', 'Explain fork/exec/wait in one breath, then contrast with Windows CreateProcess'] },
  { topic: 'Process States', title: 'Process States and Transitions', content: 'A process moves through New (being created), Ready (in memory, waiting for the CPU), Running (executing on a core), Waiting/Blocked (waiting for I/O or an event) and Terminated. Transitions: admitted (New -> Ready), dispatch (Ready -> Running), preempt/interrupt (Running -> Ready), block (Running -> Waiting on I/O), wake-up (Waiting -> Ready when I/O completes), exit (Running -> Terminated).\n\nTwo transitions are impossible: Waiting -> Running (a blocked process must become Ready and then be dispatched) and Ready -> Waiting (a process blocks only while running, by requesting I/O). Some texts add suspend/swap states for processes moved out of memory.\n\nThe dispatcher does the context switch from Ready to Running; the scheduler picks which Ready process runs next. Only one process runs per core at any instant.', keyPoints: ['Five states: New, Ready, Running, Waiting, Terminated', 'Running -> Ready happens on preemption or interrupt', 'Waiting -> Running is impossible; must pass through Ready', 'Blocking is voluntary (I/O request); preemption is involuntary', 'Scheduler chooses, dispatcher switches'], commonMistakes: ['Drawing an arrow from Waiting directly to Running', 'Confusing preemption (Running -> Ready) with blocking (Running -> Waiting)'], interviewTips: ['Draw the full diagram with all six legal transitions and label each', 'Explain why the two illegal transitions cannot happen'] },
  { topic: 'PCB', title: 'Process Control Block (PCB)', content: 'The PCB is the kernel data structure that represents a process - its resume inside the OS. It stores: process ID and parent PID, process state, program counter, CPU register snapshot, scheduling information (priority, queue pointers), memory management info (page table pointer, limits), accounting data (CPU used, time limits), I/O status and the list of open files.\n\nDuring a context switch the kernel saves the running process state into its PCB, picks the next process, and restores its state from its PCB. The switch itself is pure overhead - no useful user work happens - which is why OSes work hard to make switches fast (and why thread switches, which keep the address space, are cheaper).\n\nPCBs live in kernel-protected memory, usually linked into scheduling queues; fork() copies the PCB, which is how children inherit open files and attributes.', keyPoints: ['PCB = per-process record: PID, state, PC, registers, page table, files', 'Context switch = save state to PCB, restore next from PCB', 'Switch cost is pure overhead - no user work during it', 'Stored in kernel space, organised in queues/lists', 'fork() clones the PCB, so children inherit resources'], commonMistakes: ['Listing only two or three PCB fields - aim for at least five confidently', 'Claiming context switching saves files or heap - it saves register/PC state; memory stays put'], interviewTips: ['List five PCB fields from memory, then explain the context-switch steps in order', 'Explain why thread switching is cheaper using the shared address space'] },
  { topic: 'Threads', title: 'Threads and Thread Models', content: 'A thread is the unit of CPU execution inside a process: it has its own thread ID, program counter, register set and stack, but shares the code section, data section, heap and open files with its sibling threads. Benefits: responsiveness (a UI thread stays alive while a worker blocks), resource sharing (cheap communication through shared memory), economy (creating and switching threads costs far less than processes) and scalability (threads run in parallel on multicore).\n\nUser-level threads are managed by a library, invisible to the kernel - fast to create and switch, but one blocking system call blocks the whole process, and they cannot use multiple cores directly. Kernel-level threads are scheduled individually by the OS - true parallelism and per-thread blocking, but creation and switching cost more. Many-to-one, one-to-one and many-to-many models map user threads onto kernel threads; modern OSes use one-to-one.\n\nThe price of shared memory: races. Any shared mutable state needs synchronisation - the segue to the next topics.', keyPoints: ['Thread = own PC, registers, stack; shares code, data, heap, files', 'User-level: fast but blocking calls stop the whole process', 'Kernel-level: true parallelism, higher management cost', 'Models: many-to-one, one-to-one, many-to-many', 'Shared memory means races - synchronisation is mandatory'], commonMistakes: ['Saying threads share everything - each has a private stack, registers and PC', 'Assuming user threads scale across cores without kernel support'], interviewTips: ['Contrast user-level vs kernel-level threads with the blocking-call problem', 'Mention the many-to-one/one-to-one/many-to-many models by name'] },
  { topic: 'Process vs Thread', title: 'Process vs Thread', content: 'A process is an independent program in execution with its own protected address space; a thread is a unit of execution inside a process that shares that address space with its peers. Threads share the code section, data section, heap, open files and signal handlers, but each thread keeps a private program counter, register set, stack and thread ID. Creation, termination and context switching are much cheaper for threads because the kernel does not swap page tables and the caches stay warm.\n\nThe real trade-off is isolation versus efficiency. A crashing process does not take its siblings down, while one thread writing through a bad pointer can corrupt the entire process, so isolation favours processes and throughput favours threads. Communication matches this: processes need explicit IPC such as pipes, sockets or shared memory segments with kernel mediation, while threads talk through ordinary shared variables - and therefore must synchronise with locks to avoid races. Real systems mix both: Chrome isolates tabs as processes for safety, while a web server uses a thread or event pool for cheap concurrency.\n\nInterview framing: give the shared-versus-private table first, then the cost comparison, then the safety trade-off - that order answers almost every follow-up, including why thread switches are faster and when you would deliberately pay for processes.', keyPoints: ['Threads share code, data, heap and files; each has private stack, registers, PC', 'Process isolation means a crash cannot corrupt siblings', 'Thread creation and switching are cheaper - no address space swap', 'IPC is needed between processes; threads share memory directly', 'Browsers isolate tabs as processes; servers use thread pools'], commonMistakes: ['Saying threads share everything or nothing - be precise about the private stack and registers', 'Ignoring the safety side - shared memory means one bug can corrupt all threads'], interviewTips: ['Answer with the shared-vs-private table, then cost, then safety', 'Use a concrete example: Chrome processes vs a server thread pool'] },
  { topic: 'CPU Scheduling', title: 'CPU Scheduling Basics', content: 'CPU scheduling decides which of the ready processes gets the CPU next. The goals are maximum CPU utilisation and throughput with minimum turnaround, waiting and response time, plus fairness. The short-term scheduler picks a process on every event that can change the ready set - timer interrupts, I/O completions, forks, exits - so it must be extremely fast, while long-term schedulers control the degree of multiprogramming and medium-term schedulers swap processes out.\n\nScheduling is preemptive if the OS can forcibly take the CPU away on a timer interrupt, and non-preemptive if a process keeps the CPU until it blocks on I/O or terminates. Preemption buys responsiveness for interactive systems at the price of context-switch overhead and more races on shared data; non-preemptive scheduling is simpler but one long job delays everything else. Define the metrics precisely because interviewers ask for them: turnaround time = completion - arrival, waiting time = turnaround - burst, response time = first time on CPU - arrival, and throughput = completed jobs per unit time.\n\nA strong answer walks through: goals, metric definitions with formulas, preemptive vs non-preemptive, then the algorithm family (FCFS, SJF/SRTF, Round Robin, Priority, Multilevel Queues) and the workload each one wins on.', keyPoints: ['Short-term scheduler picks on every ready-queue-changing event', 'Preemptive = OS can revoke CPU; non-preemptive = run until block or exit', 'Turnaround = completion - arrival; waiting = turnaround - burst', 'Response time matters for interactive systems, throughput for batch', 'Preemption adds context-switch overhead but improves responsiveness'], commonMistakes: ['Confusing waiting time with turnaround time - know the formulas cold', 'Assuming preemption is always better - it costs switches and complicates shared data'], interviewTips: ['Define all four metrics with formulas before naming algorithms', 'State clearly which systems need preemption and why'] },
  { topic: 'FCFS', title: 'First Come First Serve (FCFS)', content: 'First Come First Serve is the simplest CPU scheduling algorithm: processes are executed strictly in arrival order using a FIFO ready queue, non-preemptively. Implementation is trivial - one queue, enqueue on arrival, dispatch from the head - and every job eventually runs, so there is no starvation. Its weakness is that order, not length, decides everything, so average waiting time can be terrible when a long job arrives early.\n\nThe convoy effect is the classic interview point: short jobs stuck behind one long burst wait the entire duration, like cars queued behind a slow truck. Worked example: P1 with burst 24 arrives at time 0; P2 and P3 with burst 3 each arrive at time 1. Waiting times are P1 = 0, P2 = 23, P3 = 26, average = 16.3 ms. Reverse the arrival order and the average collapses to about 3 ms - same jobs, only the order changed. FCFS also performs poorly for interactive workloads because a running job cannot be preempted, so response time is unbounded.\n\nUse FCFS answers to set up comparisons: it is the baseline that SJF improves on and that Round Robin fixes for interactivity. Mention that real batch systems and simple I/O request queues use it because of its fairness and simplicity, not performance.', keyPoints: ['Non-preemptive FIFO - run strictly in arrival order', 'Trivial implementation, no starvation', 'Convoy effect: short jobs stuck behind one long burst', 'Average waiting time depends entirely on arrival order', 'Bad response time - unusable for interactive systems'], commonMistakes: ['Forgetting FCFS is non-preemptive - no timer-based switching', 'Not being able to reproduce the convoy-effect numeric example'], interviewTips: ['Work the 24/3/3 burst example to show order dominating waiting time', 'Position FCFS as the baseline the other algorithms improve on'] },
  { topic: 'SJF', title: 'Shortest Job First (SJF)', content: 'Shortest Job First picks the ready process with the smallest CPU burst and runs it to completion, non-preemptively. Mathematically it is provably optimal for average waiting time when all processes arrive at time 0 - scheduling the shortest jobs first minimises the sum of waiting times, a classic greedy-exchange argument. Its practical problem: the length of the next burst is unknown at scheduling time.\n\nReal systems estimate the next burst with exponential averaging: predicted burst = a * actual last burst + (1 - a) * previous prediction, with a around 0.5 weighting recent behaviour heavily. This works because CPU bursts are strongly correlated process to process. The second problem is starvation: under continuous arrival of short jobs, a long job may wait indefinitely, because every newcomer with a smaller burst jumps ahead of it.\n\nA worked example with four jobs arriving at 0 with bursts 6, 8, 7, 3 gives order 3, 6, 7, 8 and waiting times 0, 3, 9, 16, average 7 ms, versus 10.5 ms under FCFS order - that contrast is the heart of a good SJF answer. SJF is a special case of priority scheduling where priority = 1 / burst time.', keyPoints: ['Optimal average waiting time among non-preemptive algorithms', 'Next burst length must be estimated - exponential averaging', 'Starvation risk for long jobs under short-job arrival streams', 'SJF = priority scheduling with priority = 1 / burst time', 'Non-preemptive: runs each job to completion'], commonMistakes: ['Claiming SJF is always optimal - only for average waiting time, and only with known bursts', 'Ignoring the starvation problem interviewers expect you to raise'], interviewTips: ['Prove optimality with the exchange argument in one sentence', 'Give the exponential averaging formula with a = 0.5'] },
  { topic: 'SRTF', title: 'Shortest Remaining Time First (SRTF)', content: 'SRTF is the preemptive version of SJF: whenever a new process arrives, the scheduler compares its burst with the remaining burst of the running process and preempts if the newcomer is shorter. This yields the provably minimum average waiting time of all algorithms, because at every instant the job with the least remaining work runs.\n\nClassic worked example - P1 (burst 8, arrives 0), P2 (4, arrives 1), P3 (9, arrives 2), P4 (5, arrives 3): P1 runs 0-1, P2 preempts and runs 1-5, P4 runs 5-10, P1 resumes 10-17, P3 runs 17-26. Waiting times: P1 = 9, P2 = 0, P3 = 15, P4 = 2, average 6.5 ms versus 7.8 ms under FCFS. Being able to draw this Gantt chart quickly is a very common interview exercise.\n\nThe costs: frequent context switches add overhead, burst times still must be estimated, and long jobs can starve exactly as in SJF, now preemptively. SRTF is therefore rarely used raw in general-purpose OSes - it survives in approximate form inside Multilevel Feedback Queues, which demote CPU hogs to lower queues instead of tracking exact remaining time.', keyPoints: ['Preemptive SJF - switch when a shorter burst arrives', 'Provably minimum average waiting time overall', 'Requires burst estimates and causes more context switches', 'Long jobs can starve preemptively', 'MLFQ is its practical approximation'], commonMistakes: ['Wrong Gantt chart - check preemption at every arrival time', 'Comparing SRTF waiting times against SJF without recomputing'], interviewTips: ['Practise the 8/4/9/5 example until the Gantt chart is automatic', 'Explain why MLFQ approximates SRTF without knowing burst lengths'] },
  { topic: 'Synchronization', title: 'Process Synchronization', content: 'Process synchronisation exists because concurrent processes sharing data produce race conditions: the final result depends on the interleaving of their executions. The canonical example is two threads running counter++ - the single statement compiles to load, increment, store, and two interleaved executions can both load the same old value and lose one increment, so the counter ends wrong. The race window sits between the read and the write; any preemption there is fatal.\n\nRaces are not only a multiprocessor problem: even on a single CPU, preemptive scheduling can switch threads between the load and the store, which is why kernel code touching shared structures must be protected everywhere. Getting it wrong produces lost updates, inconsistent reads, corrupt data structures and heisenbugs that vanish under a debugger because timing changes.\n\nThe toolbox, layered from hardware upward: disabling interrupts (kernel-only), atomic instructions such as test-and-set and compare-and-swap, mutex locks and semaphores built on those primitives, then monitors and message passing, and language-level constructs such as Java synchronized blocks. Every high-level construct compiles down to atomic hardware instructions - presenting this layered story is the backbone of a complete synchronisation answer.', keyPoints: ['Race condition: result depends on execution interleaving', 'counter++ = load, increment, store - the classic lost update', 'Preemptive uniprocessors race too - not only multicore', 'Layers: atomic instructions -> mutex/semaphore -> monitor', 'Symptoms: lost updates, inconsistent reads, corrupt structures'], commonMistakes: ['Claiming single-core systems cannot race - preemption between instructions suffices', 'Treating counter++ or x = x + 1 as atomic'], interviewTips: ['Walk the two-thread increment interleaving step by step', 'Name one atomic primitive and one high-level construct it enables'] },
  { topic: 'Critical Section', title: 'The Critical Section Problem', content: 'A critical section is a code segment that touches shared resources - variables, files, device buffers - and therefore must not be executed by more than one process at a time. Every solution has the same four-part structure: entry section (request permission), critical section, exit section (release), remainder section. Designing correct entry and exit protocols is the critical section problem.\n\nAny correct solution must satisfy three conditions. Mutual exclusion: at most one process inside at a time. Progress: if the section is free and some processes want in, one of them must be allowed in in finite time - the decision cannot be postponed forever by processes outside the section. Bounded waiting: there is a limit on how many times other processes may enter between one request and its grant, which rules out starvation. Interviewers often ask you to recite exactly these three and test with interleaving traces.\n\nSolutions span software and hardware. Petersons algorithm solves the two-process case purely in software using a flag array plus a turn variable, and satisfies all three conditions - though on modern CPUs it needs memory barriers because of instruction reordering. Hardware gives atomic test-and-set or compare-and-swap instructions that implement locks directly, and disabling interrupts works inside uniprocessor kernels. Real kernels protect their own shared structures the same way, since the kernel is just concurrent code too.', keyPoints: ['Four-part structure: entry, critical, exit, remainder', 'Three requirements: mutual exclusion, progress, bounded waiting', 'Progress forbids postponing entry decisions indefinitely', 'Bounded waiting rules out starvation', 'Petersons = software solution; test-and-set/CAS = hardware'], commonMistakes: ['Reciting only mutual exclusion and forgetting progress and bounded waiting', 'Assuming Petersons works on modern CPUs without memory barriers'], interviewTips: ['Define all three requirements and trace one interleaving that breaks a naive flag solution', 'Explain where the kernel itself needs critical sections'] },
  { topic: 'Mutex', title: 'Mutex Locks', content: 'A mutex is the simplest synchronisation primitive: a lock with two atomic operations, acquire (lock) before the critical section and release (unlock) after it. If the lock is held, the acquirer either blocks or spins, but never two owners at once. The atomicity of acquire is guaranteed underneath by hardware instructions such as test-and-set or compare-and-swap, which is why a mutex works on multiprocessors where plain flag variables fail.\n\nBlocking mutexes versus spinlocks is the key design point. A blocking mutex puts the waiting thread to sleep and context-switches it away - right when the wait is expected to be long, because spinning wastes CPU for the whole wait. A spinlock busy-waits in a tight loop - right when the wait is short (a few dozen instructions) on multiprocessors, because sleeping costs a context switch that can easily exceed the wait itself. Kernels use spinlocks in interrupt handlers and inside the scheduler where sleeping is not allowed, and user-space runtimes often use adaptive mutexes that spin briefly before blocking.\n\nMutexes usually have ownership: only the thread that acquired may release, which enables recursive locking, priority inheritance for inversion control, and clean deadlock detection. The classic bugs to name: forgetting to unlock on an error path, unlocking without ownership, and acquiring two mutexes in different orders in different threads - the textbook recipe for deadlock.', keyPoints: ['acquire/release are atomic, built on test-and-set or CAS', 'Blocking mutex for long waits, spinlock for very short ones', 'Spinlocks used where sleeping is forbidden (interrupt context)', 'Ownership enables recursion and priority inheritance', 'Forget-to-unlock and lock-order inversion are the classic bugs'], commonMistakes: ['Recommending spinlocks on single-core systems - they burn the whole quantum', 'Confusing mutex ownership with a semaphores ownerless model'], interviewTips: ['Answer blocking-vs-spinning with the wait-duration trade-off', 'Mention adaptive mutexes as what real runtimes do'] },
  { topic: 'Semaphore', title: 'Semaphores', content: 'A semaphore is an integer variable accessed only through two atomic operations: wait (P, decrements and blocks if the value would go negative) and signal (V, increments and wakes a waiter if any). Dijkstra introduced them, and they come in two flavours: binary semaphores (0 or 1, mutual exclusion) and counting semaphores (non-negative integer, guarding a pool of n identical resources such as connections or buffer slots). Unlike a mutex, a semaphore has no owner - any thread may signal it, which is exactly what makes signalling between threads possible.\n\nThe standard interview exercise is the bounded-buffer producer-consumer solution with three semaphores: mutex = 1 for mutual exclusion on the buffer, empty = n counting free slots, full = 0 counting filled slots. The producer waits on empty, locks mutex, inserts, unlocks, signals full; the consumer mirrors it with full and empty. Order matters: wait on the resource semaphore BEFORE the mutex, or the classic deadlock appears when a producer holding the mutex blocks on full. The same pattern solves readers-writers (counting active readers) and, poorly, dining philosophers.\n\nSemaphores serve two distinct jobs - mutual exclusion and sequencing or signalling between processes - and mixing the two jobs in one semaphore is a classic design error. Modern practice prefers mutexes plus condition variables for exclusion-plus-waiting, reserving semaphores for resource counting, but interviews still expect the semaphore solutions cold.', keyPoints: ['Atomic wait(P) and signal(V); blocks when value would go negative', 'Binary semaphore = mutex-like; counting = pool of n resources', 'No ownership - any thread can signal, enabling signalling', 'Producer-consumer: mutex + empty + full with strict wait order', 'Resource wait must come before mutex wait or deadlock follows'], commonMistakes: ['Waiting on mutex before empty/full in producer-consumer - deadlock', 'Using one semaphore for both exclusion and signalling duties'], interviewTips: ['Write the three-semaphore producer-consumer solution from memory', 'Explain binary vs counting with a connection-pool example'] },
  { topic: 'Deadlock', title: 'Deadlock and Its Conditions', content: 'A deadlock is a state where a set of processes are blocked forever, each holding at least one resource and waiting for a resource held by another member of the set - a closed circle of waiting with no external help. Example: P1 holds printer R1 and requests tape R2, P2 holds R2 and requests R1; neither can ever proceed. Deadlock differs from starvation: starvation is unfair waiting with the system still progressing, deadlock is permanent stopping of the involved set.\n\nFour conditions must ALL hold simultaneously for deadlock, and breaking any one prevents it: mutual exclusion (resource usable by one process at a time), hold and wait (a process holds resources while waiting for more), no preemption (resources cannot be forcibly taken), and circular wait (a cycle P0 waits for P1 waits for ... waits for P0). The resource allocation graph models this: request edges from processes to resources, assignment edges from resources to processes; a cycle is a necessary condition, and with single-instance resources a cycle is also sufficient for deadlock.\n\nSystems handle deadlock in four ways: prevention (design away a condition), avoidance (grant requests only if the state stays safe), detection and recovery (let it happen, then find and break cycles by victim rollback or killing), and the ostrich approach - ignoring it, which is what Linux and Windows do for general resources because deadlock is rare and the checks are expensive. Databases, in contrast, do full detection with transaction rollback.', keyPoints: ['Permanent blocking of a circular wait among resource holders', 'Four necessary conditions: mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption, circular wait', 'Break any one condition and deadlock becomes impossible', 'Cycle in the RAG is necessary always; sufficient if single-instance', 'General OSes ignore deadlock; databases detect and roll back'], commonMistakes: ['Listing the four conditions but claiming any two suffice', 'Confusing deadlock with starvation'], interviewTips: ['Recite all four conditions and give one prevention trick for each', 'State when a RAG cycle means deadlock: single-instance resources only'] },
  { topic: 'Deadlock Prevention', title: 'Deadlock Prevention', content: 'Deadlock prevention restains how requests are made so that at least one of the four necessary conditions can never hold. Against mutual exclusion: make resources sharable, as read-only files are - but printers and locks are inherently exclusive, so this only goes so far. Against hold and wait: require a process to request everything it will ever need at start, or to release all held resources before making any new request - a process copying a file to a printer would grab both devices upfront; the price is low utilisation and possible starvation of big requesters.\n\nAgainst no preemption: allow taking resources away from a process that is waiting, applying only to resources whose state can be saved and restored - CPU time and memory qualify, printers do not; the practical version is the OS preempting pages or the CPU, which is why those resources never deadlock. Against circular wait: impose a total ordering on resource types and require every process to request resources in strictly increasing order and release in reverse - if all code follows the order, no cycle can form. This is THE practical prevention technique: lock hierarchies in kernels, databases and libraries all implement it.\n\nThe overall cost is real: prevention lowers concurrency and device utilisation and can starve processes with large requirements. In interviews, lead with the circular-wait ordering rule, explain why lock-ordering bugs are the most common real-world deadlock, and finish with the utilisation trade-off.', keyPoints: ['Deny one of the four conditions permanently', 'Sharable resources kill mutual exclusion for that class', 'All-at-once acquisition kills hold and wait but wastes resources', 'Preemption works only for state-savable resources (CPU, memory)', 'Resource ordering kills circular wait - the industry-standard fix'], commonMistakes: ['Proposing preemption of printers - state cannot be preserved', 'Forgetting that prevention can starve processes needing many resources'], interviewTips: ['Present lock ordering as the practical answer with kernel examples', 'Admit the cost: lower utilisation and possible starvation'] },
  { topic: 'Deadlock Avoidance', title: 'Deadlock Avoidance and Safe States', content: 'Deadlock avoidance takes advance information - each process declares its maximum resource needs upfront - and the OS grants a request only if the system would remain in a safe state. A state is safe if some sequence of all processes exists in which each can obtain its maximum remaining need, finish, and return its resources; such a sequence is a safe sequence. In a safe state deadlock is impossible no matter how processes actually behave; in an unsafe state deadlock is possible but not guaranteed, since processes rarely demand their maxima.\n\nThe relationship to memorise: safe states never deadlock; every deadlocked state is unsafe; unsafe does not mean deadlocked. Avoidance is more permissive than prevention - it allows hold and wait and circular structures as long as safety holds. For single-instance resources the resource allocation graph is extended with claim edges for future requests: granting a request is refused if adding the assignment edge would create a cycle.\n\nFor multiple instances of resources, avoidance needs arithmetic rather than graph cycles, and that is the Banker algorithm - the safety check is exactly the Banker safety algorithm run on the current state. The costs that keep avoidance out of general-purpose OSes: processes rarely know their maxima in advance, the check runs on every request, and it fixes the number of resources and processes statically. It remains useful in embedded and real-time systems and as a classic interview exercise in careful state reasoning.', keyPoints: ['Grant only if the resulting state is safe', 'Safe state = an order exists where all processes can finish', 'Safe -> never deadlock; unsafe -> possible but not certain', 'Single-instance: RAG with claim edges; multi-instance: Banker', 'Needs declared maxima and per-request checks - hence rarely used'], commonMistakes: ['Equating unsafe state with deadlock - unsafe is only the possibility', 'Forgetting that avoidance allows hold-and-wait, unlike prevention'], interviewTips: ['Define safe state precisely, then place safe/unsafe/deadlocked on one line', 'Explain why maxima declarations make this impractical generally'] },
  { topic: "Banker's Algorithm", title: "The Banker's Algorithm", content: "The Banker algorithm implements deadlock avoidance for multiple resource instances. Data structures: Available[m] (free instances per resource type), Max[n][m] (declared maximum demand), Allocation[n][m] (currently held) and Need = Max - Allocation (remaining demand). When process i requests a vector, the banker first checks the request does not exceed Need and Available, then tentatively grants it and runs the safety algorithm; only a still-safe resulting state is committed, otherwise the process waits.\n\nThe safety algorithm: set Work = Available and Finish[i] = false for all i; repeatedly find any i with Finish[i] = false and Need[i] <= Work; pretend it finished by adding Allocation[i] into Work and marking Finish[i] = true; if no such i remains and some Finish is still false, the state is unsafe. In the classic textbook state with Available = (3, 3, 2) and five processes, P1s Need fits first, releasing its Allocation grows Work, then the rest fit one by one - the discovered order is the safe sequence. The mental model: lend only if some order of full repayment exists, like a bank that never lends below the level where all loans could still be repaid.\n\nComplexity is O(n * m) per request, plus the practical flaws: maxima must be declared and trusted, resource counts are fixed, and the check adds overhead everywhere. Say all of that after demonstrating the algorithm - interviewers reward the trade-off awareness as much as the mechanics.", keyPoints: ['Need = Max - Allocation; request checked against Need and Available', 'Grant tentatively, keep it only if the safety check passes', 'Safety: Work = Available; find Need <= Work, release Allocation, repeat', 'Output of the safety check is a safe sequence', 'Cost: declared maxima, O(n*m) per request, static resources'], commonMistakes: ['Forgetting Need is Max minus Allocation when filling tables', 'Declaring a state safe after checking only one process', 'Granting when Available fits the request but safety fails'], interviewTips: ['Run the safety algorithm on the textbook Available = (3,3,2) example', 'Close with why real OSes skip it: untrusted maxima and overhead'] },
  { topic: 'Memory Management', title: 'Memory Management Basics', content: 'Memory management maps the addresses processes use onto physical memory while protecting processes from each other. The MMU (memory management unit) performs this translation in hardware on every access. Address binding can happen at compile time (absolute binaries), load time (relocatable code fixed when loaded) or execution time (translation on every access, which is what virtual memory requires). Logical or virtual addresses are what the CPU generates; physical addresses are what the memory sees - the two are different as soon as relocation happens at run time.\n\nThe classical approach is contiguous allocation: each process occupies one consecutive region. With fixed partitions, memory is pre-divided and the leftover space inside a partition is internal fragmentation. With variable partitions, the OS keeps holes and allocates from them using first fit (first hole big enough), best fit (smallest adequate hole - leaves tiny useless slivers) or worst fit (largest hole - leaves large usable remainders). All variable-partition schemes suffer external fragmentation: enough total free memory exists but scattered in holes too small to use; empirical studies showed that with first fit, roughly a third to half of memory can end up unusable, the so-called 50 percent rule.\n\nTwo escapes exist: compaction - shuffle processes together to merge holes, expensive and only possible when relocation is dynamic - and abandoning contiguity entirely, which is exactly what paging and segmentation do. That transition, contiguous schemes to non-contiguous ones, is the story arc an interviewer wants to hear.', keyPoints: ['MMU translates logical to physical addresses at run time', 'Binding times: compile, load, execution; VM needs execution-time binding', 'First/best/worst fit with different fragmentation profiles', 'External fragmentation = free memory split into unusable holes', 'Internal fragmentation = waste inside an allocated unit'], commonMistakes: ['Confusing internal (inside an allocation) with external (between allocations) fragmentation', 'Claiming best fit is always better - it creates tiny unusable slivers'], interviewTips: ['Explain the 50 percent rule for external fragmentation under first fit', 'Set up why the world moved to paging'] },
  { topic: 'Paging', title: 'Paging', content: 'Paging removes external fragmentation by splitting physical memory into equal-size frames and logical memory into equal-size pages of the same size (commonly 4 KB). Any page can go into any free frame, so allocation is bookkeeping instead of searching for contiguous holes. A logical address is split by hardware into a page number (high bits) and an offset (low bits); the page number indexes the per-process page table, which yields the frame number, and frame number + offset forms the physical address. Only the last page suffers internal fragmentation, on average half a page.\n\nTranslation would need an extra memory access to read the page table, so a TLB (translation lookaside buffer, a small associative cache) caches recent translations. With TLB hit ratio h, memory access time m and TLB time t, effective access time = h * (t + m) + (1 - h) * (t + 2m); hit rates around 99 percent bring EAT close to m. Worked bit arithmetic to know cold: 32-bit addresses with 4 KB pages give 2^20 = 1M pages and a 12-bit offset; 64-bit systems need far more than a flat table can hold.\n\nThat size problem drives multi-level (hierarchical) page tables, which translate via several small tables so unused ranges cost nothing, and inverted page tables, which keep one entry per frame instead of per virtual page. Paging also enables sharing: identical read-only code pages (reentrant code) can map into many processes - the clean, protected foundation that virtual memory builds on.', keyPoints: ['Equal-size pages and frames; any page into any frame', 'Logical address = page number + offset; page table maps to frame', 'TLB caches translations; EAT = h(t+m) + (1-h)(t+2m)', '32-bit with 4 KB pages: 20-bit page number, 12-bit offset', 'Multi-level and inverted tables solve page-table size'], commonMistakes: ['Splitting address bits the wrong way - offset bits = log2(page size)', 'Forgetting the extra memory access per translation without a TLB'], interviewTips: ['Do the 2^20 pages / 12-bit offset calculation on the spot', 'Explain why a TLB hit ratio of 99 percent matters quantitatively'] },
  { topic: 'Segmentation', title: 'Segmentation', content: 'Segmentation reflects the programmer view of memory: a program is a collection of variable-size logical units - code, stack, heap, global data, maybe a symbol table - each called a segment. Each segment gets its own base and limit stored in a segment table; a logical address is the pair (segment number, offset), and the MMU checks offset < limit, then adds the base to form the physical address. Violating the limit raises a protection fault, so bounds checking is built into every access.\n\nBecause segments are variable-size, external fragmentation returns - allocating a segment needs a hole big enough, and holes accumulate exactly as in variable contiguous allocation. What segmentation buys is semantics: protection is per logical unit (code segment read-execute, data segment read-write), sharing is natural (map the same code segment into many processes), and growing structures grow their own segment without disturbing others. Paging hides the program structure inside an opaque array of pages; segmentation exposes it.\n\nModern systems use a hybrid: segments for protection and sharing semantics, with each segment paged internally - the x86 protected mode did exactly this (segmentation with paging underneath), and 64-bit modes mostly flattened segmentation away. In interviews, contrast the two on three axes: fragmentation (paging: internal only; segmentation: external), visibility (invisible vs logical units) and granularity of protection and sharing (page vs semantic segment).', keyPoints: ['Variable-size logical units: code, stack, heap, data', 'Segment table holds base + limit; limit violation faults', 'Natural per-segment protection and sharing', 'External fragmentation returns with variable-size allocation', 'Hybrid segmented paging was the classic x86 design'], commonMistakes: ['Mixing up base and limit roles in translation', 'Claiming segmentation avoids fragmentation - it trades one kind for another'], interviewTips: ['Compare paging vs segmentation on fragmentation, visibility, protection', 'Mention the x86 hybrid as the historical synthesis'] },
  { topic: 'Virtual Memory', title: 'Virtual Memory and Demand Paging', content: 'Virtual memory lets programs execute without all their pages in RAM: pages load on demand, on first touch. The page table entry carries a valid/invalid bit; an access to an invalid page traps to the OS as a page fault, and the fault handler locates the page on disk (swap space or file), allocates a frame, reads the page in, updates the page table and restarts the faulting instruction - which then succeeds. With pure demand paging a process starts with zero pages, so even a program larger than physical memory can run.\n\nThe performance equation rules everything: memory access is about 100 ns but a page fault means a disk access of several milliseconds - five orders of magnitude worse. Effective access time = (1 - p) * memory_time + p * page_fault_time; with p = 0.001 and 8 ms service, EAT = 0.999 * 100 + 0.001 * 8,000,000 = about 8.1 microseconds - 80 times slower than RAM. That is why the acceptable fault rate is tiny, why page replacement quality matters so much, and why the working set - the set of pages a process actively uses - should stay resident.\n\nBeyond running big programs, virtual memory powers core OS features: lazy loading via mmap of files and executables, copy-on-write fork where parent and child share pages until one writes, memory-mapped I/O, and overcommit with swapping cold pages to disk. Interview framing: define demand paging, walk the fault-handling steps in order, then give the EAT arithmetic - that combination shows both mechanism and consequence.', keyPoints: ['Load pages on first touch; valid bit triggers page faults', 'Fault handling: trap, locate on disk, read into frame, update table, restart instruction', 'EAT = (1-p)*mem + p*fault; faults are 10^5 times costlier than RAM hits', 'Working set = pages in active use; must stay resident', 'Enables COW fork, mmap lazy loading, swapping, overcommit'], commonMistakes: ['Forgetting the restart of the faulting instruction after servicing', 'Accepting page fault rates above a fraction of a percent'], interviewTips: ['Do the p = 0.001, 8 ms EAT calculation live', 'List the page-fault steps in order without notes'] },
  { topic: 'Round Robin', title: 'Round Robin (RR) Scheduling', content: 'Round Robin is the preemptive scheduler of time-sharing systems: each ready process gets the CPU for a fixed time quantum, then a timer interrupt forces a switch and the process goes to the tail of the circular ready queue. Its defining property is bounded response time - with n ready processes and quantum q, no process waits more than (n - 1) * q before its first slice, which is exactly what interactive systems need.\n\nThe quantum choice is the whole game. Too large and RR degenerates into FCFS with its poor responsiveness; too small and the CPU spends most of its time doing context switches instead of user work. The classic rule of thumb: choose q so that about 80 percent of CPU bursts are shorter than the quantum, typically 10-100 ms. Worked example with quantum 2 and bursts P1 = 5, P2 = 3, P3 = 1 (all arrive at 0): Gantt 0-2 P1, 2-4 P2, 4-5 P3, 5-7 P1, 7-8 P2; waiting times are 2, 5, 4, average about 3.67 ms - compute this cleanly and you have the standard RR answer.\n\nCompared with SJF, RR usually has higher average turnaround but far better response time, and it is starvation-free since every process is served within one rotation. Mention that real schedulers (Windows, Linux CFS in spirit) build on round-robin rotation within priority levels.', keyPoints: ['Fixed time quantum, preemptive, circular FIFO rotation', 'Response time bounded by (n - 1) * q', 'Small q = switch overhead; large q = becomes FCFS', 'Rule of thumb: 80 percent of bursts shorter than quantum', 'Starvation-free and fair - each process served per rotation'], commonMistakes: ['Not placing newly arrived processes consistently behind existing ones', 'Ignoring context-switch cost when the quantum is tiny'], interviewTips: ['Do the q = 2 worked example end to end with waiting times', 'Explain both failure modes of a badly chosen quantum'] },
  { topic: 'Priority Scheduling', title: 'Priority Scheduling', content: 'Priority scheduling gives each process a priority number and always runs the highest-priority ready process. It comes in a non-preemptive flavour (a running process keeps the CPU until it blocks or exits even if a higher-priority process arrives) and a preemptive flavour (the arrival of a higher-priority process preempts immediately). SJF is just priority scheduling where priority is inversely proportional to burst time, and Round Robin is commonly used as a tie-breaker among equal priorities.\n\nTwo problems must be named. First, starvation or indefinite blocking: under a stream of high-priority arrivals, the lowest-priority process may never run; the standard cure is aging - gradually raising the priority of every process the longer it waits, so old low-priority jobs eventually win. Second, priority inversion: a high-priority task blocks on a lock held by a low-priority task while medium-priority tasks keep preempting the lock holder, so the high-priority task waits on the medium ones - famously the Mars Pathfinder failure; the fix is priority inheritance, where the lock holder temporarily borrows the highest priority of its waiters.\n\nMultilevel queue and multilevel feedback queue schedulers are priority scheduling made concrete: fixed queues per priority class, with MLFQ dynamically adjusting a process level from observed behaviour, which approximates SRTF without knowing burst lengths.', keyPoints: ['Run highest-priority ready process; preemptive or non-preemptive', 'SJF = priority scheduling with priority = 1 / burst time', 'Starvation fixed by aging - raise priority with wait time', 'Priority inversion fixed by priority inheritance (Mars Pathfinder)', 'MLFQ = practical priority scheduler with dynamic levels'], commonMistakes: ['Forgetting aging as the starvation answer - it is the expected follow-up', 'Describing priority inversion incorrectly - it needs a lock, not just priorities'], interviewTips: ['Name both problems (starvation, inversion) with their standard fixes', 'Connect SJF and priority scheduling in one line'] },
  { topic: 'Page Replacement', title: 'Page Replacement Algorithms', content: 'When a page fault occurs and no frame is free, the OS must evict some resident page - the page replacement algorithm decides which. The goal is minimising the page fault rate, because each fault costs milliseconds of disk I/O against nanoseconds of memory access. Algorithms are evaluated either with reference strings by hand or with traces from real workloads; in interviews you will be handed a reference string and asked to count faults under a given algorithm with a given frame count.\n\nThe lineup: FIFO evicts the oldest resident and is trivial but suffers Belady anomaly; Optimal (OPT, Belady) evicts the page not used for the longest future time, is provably minimal, and serves as the unreachable benchmark since it needs future knowledge; LRU approximates OPT using the past, evicting the least recently used page, and has no anomaly; Clock (second chance) is the practical hardware-friendly approximation of LRU using a reference bit and a circular pointer; LFU/MFU count frequencies and behave badly on phase changes because frequency weights history too heavily. Belady anomaly means adding frames can INCREASE faults under FIFO - the standard demonstration is the reference string 1,2,3,4,1,2,5,1,2,3,4,5: 9 faults with 3 frames but 10 with 4.\n\nLRU cannot be implemented exactly in hardware cheaply (needs a counter or stack update on every access), which is why real kernels use the Clock algorithm or multi-queue variants. Frame allocation also matters: global replacement lets a process steal frames from others while local replacement keeps per-process frame counts fixed.', keyPoints: ['FIFO: oldest out; Belady anomaly possible', 'OPT: longest-future-time out; theoretical minimum, not implementable', 'LRU: least recently used out; stack property, no anomaly', 'Clock/second chance: practical LRU approximation via reference bit', 'Evaluate with reference strings: count faults per algorithm'], commonMistakes: ['Forgetting that a page READ counts as a use for future prediction in OPT/LRU', 'Claiming more frames can hurt under LRU - anomaly is FIFO-specific'], interviewTips: ['Trace 1,2,3,4,1,2,5,1,2,3,4,5 to show the Belady anomaly', 'Explain why Clock beats exact LRU in hardware cost'] },
  { topic: 'FIFO', title: 'FIFO Page Replacement', content: 'FIFO page replacement evicts the page that has been in memory longest, tracked with a simple queue: on a fault the page at the head is removed, the new page enters at the tail. Implementation is trivial - a linked list and a pointer - which is its only real advantage.\n\nIts flaws are instructive. It ignores usage: a heavily used page that simply arrived early gets evicted first, hurting the fault rate. Worse, it suffers Belady anomaly - with some reference strings, increasing the number of frames INCREASES the number of faults, violating the intuition that more memory cannot be worse. The canonical trace is 1,2,3,4,1,2,5,1,2,3,4,5: with 3 frames, faults = 9; with 4 frames, faults = 10. Being able to run both traces side by side on paper is the standard interview exercise. LRU and OPT have the stack property (the pages resident with n frames are a subset of those with n+1) which is exactly what FIFO lacks, and that property is why they cannot show the anomaly.\n\nBecause of these problems, FIFO appears in real systems mostly as a component: second-chance (Clock) is FIFO plus a reference bit that gives pages another pass when their bit is set, and that one bit fixes most of the worst behaviours while keeping the cheap queue structure.', keyPoints: ['Evict the oldest resident page - pure queue order', 'Trivial implementation, ignores frequency of use', 'Belady anomaly: more frames can mean more faults', 'Trace 1,2,3,4,1,2,5,1,2,3,4,5: 9 faults at 3 frames, 10 at 4', 'Second chance = FIFO + reference bit, the practical fix'], commonMistakes: ['Getting the anomaly trace wrong - practise it until automatic', 'Believing FIFO approximates LRU - arrival order is not recency'], interviewTips: ['Run both frame counts of the anomaly trace without notes', 'Explain the stack property and why FIFO lacks it'] },
  { topic: 'LRU', title: 'Least Recently Used (LRU) Replacement', content: 'LRU evicts the page that has gone unused for the longest time, betting that the recent past predicts the near future - the pages a program just used are the ones it will likely use again, so the stalest page is the safest victim. It is the best practical approximation of the unimplementable Optimal algorithm, which evicts the page needed furthest in the future.\n\nLRU has the stack property: the set of pages resident with n frames is always a subset of the resident set with n+1 frames. That property makes it immune to the Belady anomaly that plagues FIFO. On the classic reference string 7,0,1,2,0,3,0,4,2,3,0,3,2,1,2,0,1,7,0,1 with 3 frames, LRU produces 12 faults versus 15 for FIFO and the theoretical minimum of 9 for OPT - quoting these three numbers together makes a complete comparison answer.\n\nThe catch is implementation cost. Exact LRU needs a hardware counter stamping every memory access (evict the page with the oldest stamp) or a stack of pages updated on every reference - both are too slow and expensive to build directly. Real systems approximate it: the Clock (second chance) algorithm keeps one reference bit per frame set by hardware on access and sweeps a pointer clearing bits and evicting clean unreferenced pages, and Linux goes further with active/inactive lists and periodic aging so that recency is tracked statistically rather than exactly.', keyPoints: ['Evict the page unused for the longest time - recency heuristic', 'Stack property -> no Belady anomaly', 'Classic string: LRU 12 faults vs FIFO 15 vs OPT 9 (3 frames)', 'Exact LRU needs counters or a reference stack - too costly in hardware', 'Clock and Linux active/inactive lists are the practical approximations'], commonMistakes: ['Evicting by arrival time (FIFO behaviour) when asked for LRU', 'Forgetting to count a hit on an already-resident page when stepping a trace'], interviewTips: ['Produce the 12/15/9 comparison on the classic 20-reference string', 'Explain why hardware cannot do exact LRU and what it does instead'] },
  { topic: 'Thrashing', title: 'Thrashing', content: 'Thrashing is the collapse state of virtual memory: a process spends more time paging than executing, because it does not have enough frames to hold its current working set. The vicious cycle: memory is over-committed, so the replacement policy keeps evicting pages that are about to be used; faults skyrocket; the CPU idles while processes wait on the disk, so utilisation drops; the long-term scheduler sees a free CPU and admits MORE processes, taking frames away from everyone and making faults worse still. Throughput craters even though the system is technically running.\n\nThe working set model explains and cures it. Define WS(t, delta) as the set of pages referenced in the most recent delta references - the locality a process is actively using. If every resident process is guaranteed at least its working set size in frames, thrashing is impossible; if the sum of working set sizes exceeds physical memory, the OS must suspend or swap out entire processes until it fits. The page fault frequency alternative measures directly: set an upper and lower bound on each process fault rate, grant frames when the rate is too high and reclaim when too low - localising the policy without tracking the working set explicitly.\n\nSymptoms an interviewer may ask about: high page fault rate, disk near 100 percent busy, CPU utilisation LOW (the counterintuitive one), and a rising multiprogramming degree. Remedies in order: reduce the degree of multiprogramming, give the working sets room, or add memory - never blame the CPU.', keyPoints: ['More time paging than executing - system collapse state', 'Trigger: frames below working set; over-committed memory', 'Feedback loop: low CPU use -> more processes -> fewer frames each', 'Working set model: guarantee |WS| frames or swap processes out', 'PFF: bound the fault rate instead of tracking the working set'], commonMistakes: ['Expecting high CPU utilisation during thrashing - it plummets', 'Treating thrashing as a CPU scheduling problem rather than memory pressure'], interviewTips: ['Draw the fault-rate versus multiprogramming-degree curve', 'Explain the working set with delta and why sum of WS sizes bounds the process count'] },
  { topic: 'File Systems', title: 'File Systems', content: 'A file system organises persistent storage: it turns raw blocks into named files with attributes (name, size, timestamps, owner, permissions), directories that map names to files, and the metadata records that hold it all - the FCB, called an inode in Unix, storing size, ownership, timestamps, permission bits, link counts and, crucially, the block pointers to the data. Directories range from single-level and two-level to trees and acyclic graphs; Unix hard links increment an inode link count so several names share one file, while symbolic links are separate files holding a path. The mount mechanism grafts one file system tree onto a directory of another, and the VFS layer lets multiple file system types coexist under one interface.\n\nThree allocation methods define the design space. Contiguous allocation gives a file consecutive blocks: superb sequential and even random access, but external fragmentation and the need to pre-size or relocate files make it impractical for general use. Linked allocation chains blocks with next-pointers: no external fragmentation and easy growth, but random access is O(n) and pointer fields steal space; the FAT is the classic fix, keeping all pointers in one table in memory. Indexed allocation puts all block pointers of a file into an index block - the Unix inode scales this with 12 direct pointers, then single, double and triple indirect blocks, covering huge files while keeping small files cheap. Free space is tracked with a bitmap (fast contiguity search) or a linked list of free blocks.\n\nPerformance and reliability complete the picture: the buffer cache keeps hot blocks in RAM, read-ahead prefetches for sequential access, and writes are ordered so crashes stay consistent - journaling file systems such as ext4 and NTFS log metadata (and optionally data) to a write-ahead journal so recovery after a crash replays committed operations instead of running a full consistency check.', keyPoints: ['Inode/FCB: metadata plus block pointers; hard links share one inode', 'Contiguous: fast but fragments; linked: grows freely, slow random access', 'Indexed (inode): direct + indirect pointers scale small to huge files', 'Free space: bitmap vs free list; buffer cache and read-ahead for speed', 'Journaling: write-ahead log of metadata for crash recovery'], commonMistakes: ['Confusing hard links (same inode, count-based) with symlinks (a path file)', 'Forgetting that linked allocation kills random access'], interviewTips: ['Sketch the inode with direct and indirect pointer levels and compute a max file size', 'Explain what journaling buys after a power failure'] },
];